          ":question/problem": "Which of the following augmented matrices have coefficient matrices that are **not** in row echelon form? Treat the final column of each augmented matrix as its constants column.\n\nSelect all that apply.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "A coefficient matrix, obtained by omitting the final column of its augmented matrix, is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The coefficient matrix of $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n- The coefficient matrix of $B$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entries of its first two rows are in columns $3$ and $2,$ respectively (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 0 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 & 5\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n- The coefficient matrix of $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccccc|c}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ has a coefficient matrix that is **not** in row echelon form."
