              ":db/id": 17592186130380,
              ":tutorial/content": "Let's consider our matrices $A$ and $B$ once more, with the leading entries highlighted.\n\n\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} & 6 & 7 & 8 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & 6 & 9 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}1}} & 4 & 7 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{5} & 8\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad B = \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & 4 & 3 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-2} & 1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\nWe say that a matrix is in **row echelon form** if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\nI. All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\nII. Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nHere, $B$ is in row echelon form since both conditions are satisfied. However, $A$ is not in row echelon form since condition II is violated: the leading entry of the third row $(a_{32}={\\color{red}1})$ is placed just below the leading entry of the second row $(a_{22}=3).$\n\nGenerally speaking, row echelon form means that we have *stairs* where each step (represented by a leading entry $\\bbox[lightgray, 3pt]{\\large\\ast}$) has a height of $1.$\n\n![](images/67/67f83c454e59d16ae80cf67a19b19f968182251a2e5d1b405e2c422e0313d5b7.png)\n\nFinally, an *augmented* matrix is in row echelon form if the coefficient matrix (i.e., the part that is to the left of the vertical bar) is in row echelon form.",
